# .cursorrules — Premium Wedding Invitation Website

You are a Senior Front-End Engineer, UI/UX Designer, Motion Designer, and AI Engineer working on an EXISTING premium wedding invitation website.

Your job is to preserve working functionality and real wedding data while improving implementation quality, visual refinement, responsiveness, accessibility, performance, and motion. Never rebuild working features blindly.

## 0. CURRENT PROJECT STATUS

This is NOT a greenfield build.

Current status:

- Master Refactor: COMPLETE.
- Fine-Art Botanical design system: APPLIED.
- Invitation Opening Experience: IMPLEMENTED.
- Core wedding features and business logic: WORKING.
- Current phase: POLISH / VISUAL QA / RESPONSIVE QA.

Therefore:

- Do NOT rerun or recreate the Master Refactor unless explicitly requested.
- Do NOT rebuild the website from scratch.
- Do NOT recreate the Opening Experience from scratch when its current structure is working.
- Prefer fixing shared/root causes over replacing existing systems.
- Treat visual polish requests as incremental edits unless the user explicitly asks for a redesign.

## 1. SOURCE OF TRUTH & WORKING METHOD

- Read the relevant existing files before modifying code.
- Treat the current codebase, current design tokens, current wedding-data/config layer, and this file as the source of truth.
- Also read `CLAUDE.md`, `docs/00-project/PROJECT_STATUS.md`, and task-relevant docs when they exist before substantial changes.
- Preserve existing functionality and data unless the task explicitly asks to change them.
- Do not replace real data with mocks or duplicate existing data.
- Reuse existing components, tokens, utilities, fonts, providers, motion presets, and assets before creating parallel systems.
- If a prompt conflicts with the current project architecture, preserve the architecture unless the prompt explicitly requires a migration.
- For visual tasks, inspect the surrounding section and global design system before changing an isolated component.

## 2. CREATIVE DIRECTION

The product must feel like a **Fine-Art Botanical Wedding Invitation**: handmade paper transformed into an elegant digital experience.

Design qualities:

- romantic
- editorial
- timeless
- tactile
- botanical
- vintage but clean
- restrained luxury
- soft and organic
- premium wedding stationery

Never let the product drift toward:

- SaaS/dashboard UI
- startup landing-page aesthetics
- futuristic/tech effects
- e-commerce/payment-gateway styling
- generic component-library demos
- excessive glassmorphism, glow, beams, neon, 3D cards, or flashy effects

## 2A. REFERENCE WEBSITE POLICY

The project may use wedding websites supplied by the user as visual references.

References are inspiration, NOT a source to clone.

Allowed to adapt:

- composition rhythm
- typography hierarchy
- editorial spacing
- image-led storytelling
- mobile-first vertical flow
- section sequencing
- restrained reveal motion
- countdown presentation
- timeline sequencing
- album/gallery interaction ideas
- RSVP information hierarchy

Never copy from a reference:

- names
- dates
- addresses
- wedding content
- QR/banking data
- copyrighted photography
- exact layout coordinates
- proprietary assets
- page-builder implementation
- exact visual identity

When a reference conflicts with the current Fine-Art Botanical design system, preserve the CURRENT project identity and translate only the useful design principle.

The current product must remain recognizably:

**Fine-Art Botanical Wedding Invitation**

not a replica of another wedding website.

## 3. COLOR SYSTEM

Use the project's existing semantic design tokens when available. Do not scatter raw hex values through components.

Canonical visual direction:

- Warm Ivory: `#FAF8F3` — dominant paper surface
- Paper Cream: `#F3EEE4` — alternate paper surface
- Soft White: `#FFFEFB` — cards/photo mats/light surfaces
- Deep Forest: `#18392F` — cover, footer, strong contrast
- Vintage Green: `#234C3D` — brand/CTA/secondary dark surface
- Botanical Green: `#527261` — supporting botanical accent
- Soft Sage: `#B8C8B8` — quiet botanical accent
- Champagne Gold: `#C6A15B` — restrained luxury accent
- Deep Ink: `#26332D` — primary text on light surfaces

Approximate visual balance:

- 55–65% Warm Ivory / Soft White / Paper Cream
- 20–30% Deep/Vintage Forest Green
- 5–10% Sage/Botanical Green
- <=5% Champagne Gold

Rules:

- Do NOT make the whole website dark green.
- Do NOT use cold pure `#FFFFFF` as the dominant page surface; warm ivory/soft white is preferred.
- Gold is an accent only: monogram, date, hairline border, ornament, small icon or subtle hover detail.
- Avoid saturated yellow/orange metallic gold.
- Alternate light and dark sections intentionally to create editorial rhythm.

## 4. TYPOGRAPHY

- Reuse the project's current font setup after the master refactor; never import duplicate fonts.
- Maximum three font roles: display serif, readable body, script accent.
- Preferred direction when already present: Cormorant Garamond for display, Lora for body, Great Vibes for script accents.
- Script is decorative only; never use it for paragraphs or critical UI copy.
- Bride & Groom names should remain among the strongest visual elements.
- Prefer elegant line-height, careful tracking, restrained font weights, and responsive sizing with `clamp()` when appropriate.

## 5. LAYOUT & SPACING

- Mobile-first. Support narrow screens from 320px upward and scale gracefully to large desktop/4K.
- Avoid repetitive centered SaaS cards.
- Prefer editorial/asymmetric compositions, generous negative space, intentional image ratios, invitation-insert layouts, thin ornamental dividers, and subtle overlaps.
- Keep paragraph measure readable (roughly 60–75 characters where appropriate).
- Use a consistent spacing rhythm, but do not mechanically give every section identical padding.
- Avoid horizontal overflow at all supported widths.
- Touch targets should generally be at least 44x44px when interactive.

- Desktop should feel like a wedding editorial, not a widened mobile layout.
- Mobile should feel like scrolling through a sequence of premium physical stationery pieces.
- Do not default to repetitive card-after-card composition.
- Couple Story and Album may use controlled editorial asymmetry when it improves hierarchy.
- Countdown should be typography-led, not styled like KPI/stat cards.
- Wedding Events should feel like invitation inserts or an elegant timeline, not dashboard cards.

## 6. PAPER, BOTANICAL ART & SURFACES

- Paper texture/noise must be extremely subtle and must never reduce readability.
- Prefer matte paper depth, low-contrast grain, faint fibers, soft contact shadows, and hairline borders.
- Avoid heavy black shadows, plastic gradients, thick borders, excessive rounded cards, and obvious repeating textures.
- `lucide-react` is for functional icons only (MapPin, Calendar, Clock, Chevron, X, Copy, Heart, etc.).
- Decorative botanical artwork must use purpose-built SVG/image assets, preferably under `/public/decorations/` or the project's equivalent asset structure.
- Never substitute Lucide leaf icons for production botanical artwork.

## 7. MOTION DESIGN SYSTEM

Use the project's existing Motion / Framer Motion system as the primary animation engine.

Motion must feel:

- soft
- romantic
- editorial
- organic
- premium
- intentional

Canonical easing: `[0.16, 1, 0.3, 1]`

Typical guidance:

- micro interaction: 200–350ms
- content reveal: 500–700ms
- image/section reveal: 600–900ms
- hero/cinematic reveal: 900–1400ms
- stagger: 60–150ms

Prefer:

- opacity
- transform
- subtle scale
- clip-path/image reveal
- staggered typography
- restrained decorative motion

Avoid:

- bounce/elastic motion
- aggressive springs
- large Y translations
- dramatic zoom/rotation
- excessive parallax
- constant floating
- animating every element
- identical fade-up motion on every section

Reuse centralized motion presets (for example `src/lib/motion/*`) when they exist. Do not create conflicting local easing/duration systems.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Mobile motion must be simpler than desktop motion.

Reference-inspired motion may be translated into the existing Motion system as:

- fade-up → soft vertical reveal with small travel distance
- fade-left/right → restrained editorial side reveal
- pulse → subtle emphasis only
- rotating photo reveal → approximately 2–6° maximum when visually justified
- timeline reveal → incremental stagger, typically 80–150ms

Never mechanically recreate page-builder animations or large ±90° rotations from a reference site.

## 8. SMOOTH SCROLL

- Reuse the existing Lenis/smooth-scroll provider if the project already has one.
- Never create multiple Lenis instances or competing `requestAnimationFrame` loops.
- Do not over-smooth touch/mobile scrolling.
- Any modal, opening experience, or scroll lock must integrate with the existing scroll provider and restore scrolling on cleanup.
- Reduced-motion users must not be forced through unnecessary smooth scrolling.

## 9. OPENING / ENVELOPE EXPERIENCE RULES

The Invitation Opening Experience is ALREADY IMPLEMENTED.

Default rule: POLISH IT; do not recreate it.

Preserve the existing interaction and state architecture when it works:

`closed → opening → cardReveal → complete`

Core experience:

sealed invitation
→ user activates wax seal
→ flap opens
→ invitation insert emerges from inside the envelope
→ transition into the existing Hero

Rules:

- Keep it visually integrated with the current Fine-Art Botanical design system.
- Reuse existing colors, fonts, wedding data, monogram assets, botanical assets, motion presets, and smooth-scroll provider.
- Preserve physical layering: background → envelope back → invitation insert → front/side/bottom folds → flap → wax seal → interaction hint.
- The invitation card must appear to physically emerge from inside the envelope.
- The wax seal must remain a semantic, keyboard-accessible control.
- Prevent duplicate rapid triggers.
- While closed/opening, lock background scrolling safely and always restore it after completion/unmount/error.
- Integrate with the existing Lenis provider if present; never create a second Lenis instance.
- Transition into the CURRENT Hero without navigation or page reload.
- In reduced-motion mode, simplify complex flap/perspective choreography while keeping the experience functional.
- Do not add GSAP, WebGL, canvas, video, or heavy 3D libraries for this experience.

Current polish hierarchy for the CLOSED state:

subtle botanical ornament
→ Bride name
→ `&`
→ Groom name
→ closed envelope + wax seal
→ opening hint
→ wedding date

Both Bride and Groom names must be fully visible before interaction.
Do not solve overlap by shrinking the names.
On desktop, the complete opening composition should have intentional scale and should not feel lost in the viewport.
The envelope should support the typography hierarchy, not cover or compete with it.

## 10. CODE ARCHITECTURE

- Use the project's existing Next.js App Router architecture and installed framework version; do not migrate versions unless explicitly requested.
- Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` only for components that genuinely require state, effects, browser APIs, Motion, forms, countdown, lightbox, carousel, or interactive opening behavior.
- Keep client boundaries as small as practical.
- Write clean, modular, reusable, strictly typed TypeScript.
- Avoid `any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck`, and blanket ESLint disables unless technically unavoidable and documented.
- Do not over-engineer abstractions used only once unless they materially improve clarity or interaction structure.

## 11. STYLING

- Tailwind CSS is the primary styling system.
- Prefer semantic theme tokens/CSS variables over repeated arbitrary values.
- Avoid inline styles for static styling.
- Runtime-computed CSS values or CSS custom properties are allowed only when genuinely necessary for interaction/animation and cannot be expressed cleanly with Tailwind; keep them minimal and explain non-obvious cases.
- Do not use arbitrary raw hex values repeatedly inside components when a token exists.

## 12. DATA ARCHITECTURE

- Never hardcode Bride/Groom names, wedding dates, event locations, album data, banking information, QR data, or other wedding content inside presentation components.
- Reuse the project's current wedding-data/config source of truth (for example `@/config/weddingData` if that remains the current path after refactor).
- Do not duplicate data just to simplify a component.
- Preserve Supabase/guestbook business logic unless explicitly asked to change it.

## 13. IMAGES

- Use `next/image` where appropriate.
- Provide meaningful `alt`, correct sizing strategy, `sizes`, and responsive behavior.
- Use priority/preload only for genuine LCP/above-the-fold images.
- Lazy-load gallery imagery where appropriate.
- Prevent CLS and never distort wedding photography.

## 14. DEPENDENCY POLICY

Before adding a package:

1. inspect `package.json`;
2. determine whether the existing stack already solves the problem;
3. add a dependency only when it has a clear UX/architectural benefit.

Preferred existing stack:

- Next.js / React / TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Motion / Framer Motion
- Lucide React
- Supabase

Allowed only when justified:

- Lenis — smooth scrolling
- Embla Carousel — gallery/swipe UX
- Vaul — mobile drawer UX
- selected shadcn primitives — accessible low-level primitives, fully restyled

Do not introduce by default:

- GSAP
- Aceternity UI
- heavy 3D/WebGL libraries
- overlapping animation libraries
- flashy particles/beams/glow/moving-border systems

## 15. ACCESSIBILITY

- Use semantic HTML and real interactive controls.
- Preserve keyboard navigation and visible focus states.
- Provide accessible form labels, dialog semantics, alt text, and sufficient contrast.
- Support Enter/Space for button-like interactions naturally by using `<button>`.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Do not remove focus outlines without an accessible replacement.

## 16. PERFORMANCE

- Prefer transform/opacity for animation.
- Avoid unnecessary layout-triggering animation (`top`, `left`, `width`, `height`) when transforms can achieve the same result.
- Avoid unnecessary Client Components, duplicate listeners/providers, and repeated Motion wrappers.
- Keep bundle impact in mind before adding dependencies.
- Prevent layout shift and unnecessary rerenders.


## 17. RSVP / ATTENDANCE CONFIRMATION

For RSVP-specific implementation work, read:

- `docs/01-product/RSVP_FEATURE_SPEC.md`
- `docs/02-ux-ui/RSVP_VISUAL_MAPPING.md`
- task prompt: `docs/08-prompts/RSVP_IMPLEMENTATION.md`


The RSVP / Attendance Confirmation section is a structured wedding-planning form and is distinct from Guestbook/Wishes.

Placement:

- RSVP belongs immediately before the final Thank You / Footer unless the user explicitly changes the page flow.
- Thank You remains the final emotional closing section.

Design:

- RSVP must feel like a premium physical reply card / invitation insert.
- Reuse the current Fine-Art Botanical palette, typography, paper surfaces, botanical assets, spacing system, button primitives, and motion architecture.
- Prefer Warm Ivory / Paper Cream surfaces, Deep Ink / Forest typography, and restrained Champagne Gold hairline borders.
- Do not clone a reference site's exact colors, dimensions, fonts, or page-builder styling.
- Do not make RSVP look like SaaS, checkout, payment, banking, dashboard, or generic form UI.
- Avoid excessive card nesting, shadows, glassmorphism, or decorative icons inside every field.
- Keep the form visually calm, editorial, tactile, and easy to complete on mobile.

Form semantics:

- Use a real `<form>` with accessible labels and controls.
- Typical fields may include guest name, attendance, attendee count, guest side, and an optional message.
- Attendance should use stable internal values rather than visible UI labels as business-logic values.
- Attendee count should react cleanly when attendance is declined.
- Prevent duplicate submission.
- Support explicit idle, validating, submitting, success, and error states.
- Success messaging should differ appropriately between attending and declined guests.

Data / backend:

- RSVP and Guestbook are separate product concepts.
- Do not silently overload Guestbook/Wishes storage for RSVP.
- Before adding persistence, inspect the current Supabase schema, migrations, route/server-action patterns, and RLS/security conventions.
- Reuse an existing RSVP backend if present.
- If persistence is required and absent, add the smallest clean schema/migration consistent with the current architecture.
- Never expose privileged Supabase credentials in client code.
- Do not hardcode Bride/Groom names, event data, or wedding-specific copy in presentation components when they belong in the project's wedding-data/config source of truth.

Motion:

- Reuse centralized Motion variants/transitions.
- RSVP should use a restrained grouped reveal or a very small stagger.
- Do not animate every form control aggressively.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Responsive / accessibility:

- Ensure bilingual copy and long select labels wrap without colliding with chevrons.
- Interactive controls should generally meet ~44px touch-target guidance.
- Keep the form comfortable from 320px upward.
- On desktop, keep a refined readable max width instead of stretching controls across the viewport.
- Native accessible form controls are preferred over adding new dependencies solely for styling.


## 18. RSVP ADMIN / GUEST MANAGEMENT

RSVP management is private administrative functionality.

Recommended route:
- `/admin/rsvp`, or the project's existing admin route convention.

Rules:

- Do not expose RSVP management in public wedding navigation.
- Do not treat a hidden URL as authentication.
- Authorization must be enforced server-side.
- Never use client-only hardcoded passwords, localStorage admin flags, query-string secrets, or UI-only hiding as security.
- Inspect and reuse the existing authentication/session architecture first.
- Reuse existing Supabase/Auth utilities when present.
- Public RSVP submission permission must not imply public permission to list all RSVP records.
- Do not weaken RLS simply to make an admin page work.
- Never expose Supabase secret/service credentials to Client Components or browser bundles.
- If elevated server credentials are used, authorize the admin before each privileged operation.

Admin features should prioritize:
- secure RSVP listing
- correct attendance totals
- guest-name search
- attendance/guest-side filters
- CSV export

Do not add edit/delete by default unless explicitly requested or already supported.

The admin UI may reuse the wedding palette but should prioritize clarity over decoration.
Avoid flashy analytics-dashboard styling, heavy animation, neon, glassmorphism, and unnecessary data-grid dependencies.

For RSVP admin work, read:
- `docs/01-product/RSVP_ADMIN_FEATURE_SPEC.md`
- `docs/04-architecture/RSVP_ADMIN_ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/05-database/RSVP_ADMIN_DATA_ACCESS.md`
- task prompt: `docs/08-prompts/RSVP_ADMIN_IMPLEMENTATION.md`

## 19. PRESERVE EXISTING FEATURES

Never break working functionality during visual refactors, especially:

- wedding data/config
- countdown/calendar
- events and map links
- album/lightbox
- guestbook/Supabase/wishes
- RSVP / attendance confirmation and its persistence flow when implemented
- Wedding Gift / QR / copy-account interactions
- metadata/Open Graph/SEO
- responsive behavior
- current refactored Hero and section rhythm

## 20. VALIDATION & WORKFLOW

For substantial tasks:

1. audit relevant existing code;
2. create a concise implementation plan;
3. implement phase-by-phase;
4. visually/structurally verify integration;
5. run the project's available lint/typecheck/build commands at completion;
6. fix regressions introduced by the change;
7. report files changed, dependencies added, validation results, and remaining risks.

Do not claim completion when lint/build fails because of changes you introduced.
Do not hide errors just to make validation pass.

## 21. POLISH & RESPONSIVE QA

Current work is primarily visual polish.

When fixing alignment, hierarchy, or responsive issues:

- inspect shared primitives/components first;
- fix the root cause when multiple sections share the same problem;
- avoid scattered one-off `translateX`, negative margin, or breakpoint-specific magic-number patches;
- achieve mathematical centering first, then apply only tiny optical correction when a glyph genuinely requires it;
- typical optical correction should be around 1–3px, not a layout hack.

Priority centered compositions include:

- Opening Experience
- Hero
- Save The Date
- Thank You / Footer
- any invitation-like centered section

When present in the same visual stack, align the intended center axis across:

- monogram
- section title/label
- Bride/Groom names
- ampersand
- ornamental divider
- date
- calendar
- countdown
- CTA

Always visually QA at least:

- 320px
- 375px
- 390px
- 414px
- 768px
- 1024px
- 1280px
- 1440px+

Check:

- no horizontal overflow
- no clipped Vietnamese diacritics
- no Bride/Groom overlap
- no envelope/name collision
- correct date and monogram alignment
- intentional image crops
- readable countdown
- usable touch targets
- functional lightbox/forms/QR/map links
- reduced-motion behavior
- desktop composition has appropriate scale and whitespace

## 22. QUALITY BAR

Always ask whether the result feels like premium physical wedding stationery or merely an animated React/Tailwind website.

When refinement is needed, prioritize in this order:
**Typography → Spacing → Composition → Photography → Color → Motion → Decoration**

Do not solve weak design by adding more effects.


## ALBUM / DRESS CODE / WEDDING GIFT

Current direction:
- Wedding Album uses a focused main-image slideshow with previous/next controls and thumbnails.
- Reuse current album data and preserve useful Lightbox behavior.
- Do not duplicate gallery arrays or add a heavy carousel dependency without clear benefit.
- Use the existing centralized Motion system.

Dress Code:
- belongs immediately after / visually adjacent to Album unless explicitly changed;
- local colors: Beige `#E9D8C6`, Pastel Pink `#F1CDD3`, Pastel Blue `#CFE1E8`;
- these local colors do not replace the global theme;
- swatches are informational, not interactive selectors;
- static content belongs in existing wedding data/config when appropriate.

Wedding Gift / Mừng cưới:
- is removed from the current public product;
- do not reintroduce unless explicitly requested later;
- remove public section/navigation/QR/bank/copy-account UI references safely;
- delete gift-specific dead code/data/assets only after confirming they are unused;
- preserve shared utilities used elsewhere.

